==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|pétrifier}}, from {{der|en|ML.|petrificāre}}, from {{der|en|la|petra||rock}}, from {{der|la|grc|πέτρα||rock}} + {{m|la|-ficāre}}, from {{m|la|facere||do, make}}, equivalent to {{con|en|petro|ify}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɛ.tɹəˌfaɪ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-petrify.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Verb===
[[Image:Petrified tree Kirstenbosch.jpg|thumb|petrified tree Kirstenbosch Cape Town]]
{{en-verb}}

# To harden [[organic]] matter by [[permeate|permeating]] with water and depositing dissolved [[mineral]]s.
#* '''1799''', {{w|Richard Kirwan}}, ''Geological Essays''
#*: a river that '''petrifies''' any sort of wood or leaves
# To produce rigidity akin to stone.
# To [[immobilize]] with [[fright]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become stone, or of a stony hardness, as organic matter by calcareous deposits.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To become stony, [[callous]], or [[obdurate]].
#* '''1685''', {{w|John Dryden}}, ''Threnodia Augustalis''
#*: Like Niobe we marble grow, / And '''petrify''' with grief.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1837|author={{w|Letitia Elizabeth Landon}}|title=Ethel Churchill|volume=1|page=233|text=Hopes, feelings, and passion, '''petrify''' one after another; the crust of experience soon hardens over the hidden past; and who, looking on the levelled and subdued exterior, could dream of the wreck and ravage that lies below?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} To make callous or obdurate; to stupefy; to paralyze; to transform; as by petrification.
#* {{RQ:Pope Dunciad|passage='''petrify''' a genius to a dunce}}
#* '''1874''', {{w|George Eliot}}, ''letter to Mrs. Ponsonby''
#*: A hideous fatalism, which ought, logically, to '''petrify''' your volition.

